>> victory condition" is not valid.
> 
> Nethack is a game. The *primary* purpose of the game is to entertain.

And the way a game entertains is by providing a goal, a set of
challenges for the player to overcome, and a number of constraints under
which the player must perform those challenges.

The goal is ascension.
The challenges are numerous -- insert description of Nethack here.
The constraints are also numerous.

If the challenges are too steep, the game becomes frustrating.
If the constraints are too harsh, the same occurs.

If the challenges are too shallow or the constraints insufficiently
constraining, though, the game tends towards boring.

Any of these can be classed as "flawed design", and while a flawed
design may not necessarily make the game 'not fun', that doesn't mean it
shouldn't still be fixed.

> the majority of players (or at least the majority of the DevTeam)
> preferred rummaging around in the dungeon to ascending, I think the
> "primary victory condition" would cease to be ascension. 

And yet this is not the case, as is patently obvious, for either the
majority of players _or_ the Dev Team; the primary victory condition has
not changed in a long time, not since the concept of "ascension"
replaced simply "escape the dungeon with the amulet of Yendor".

> your own conclusions as to how likely that is :), the point is that
> the game design, and the relative importance of the "primary victory
> condition", are subject to revision pending the desires and
> preferences of the public and / or maintainers thereof.

